  "enhanced_information": "The patient presents with symptoms and laboratory findings suggestive of hemolytic anemia, including fever, abdominal pain, jaundice, pallor, and the presence of bite cells and Heinz bodies on the peripheral smear. These findings are typically associated with oxidative stress on red blood cells, often seen in Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency. However, the normal G6PD levels in this patient suggest an alternative cause. The recent use of anti-malaria prophylaxis, which can include drugs like primaquine, is known to induce hemolysis in individuals with G6PD deficiency, but can also cause oxidative stress in some individuals without the deficiency. This points towards a drug-induced hemolytic anemia as the most likely diagnosis. The retrieved evidence paths broadly associate symptoms like pallor and jaundice with various diseases but do not specifically support or contradict the diagnosis of drug-induced hemolytic anemia. Therefore, the most consensus-aligned diagnosis, given the clinical presentation and normal G6PD levels, is drug-induced hemolytic anemia, likely due to the recent anti-malaria prophylaxis.",
